---
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Does everyone with cancer need biomarker testing?', answer: 'No. Whether a test is useful depends on the cancer type, stage, treatment options and the evidence for that specific use. Ask your oncology team which tests are recommended in your situation.' },
  { question: 'Can a liquid biopsy replace a tissue biopsy?', answer: 'Sometimes a blood-based test can answer a particular clinical question, but it does not routinely replace tissue diagnosis. A blood test can miss a change if too little tumour material is present. Your team may still recommend a tissue biopsy.' },
  { question: 'Does finding a target mean a medicine will work?', answer: 'No. A result may identify a possible treatment, but response is not guaranteed. Evidence, approved indications, other cancer features, your health and access to treatment all matter.' },
  { question: 'Are tumour tests the same as inherited-risk tests?', answer: 'No. Tumour testing looks for changes associated with the cancer. Inherited-risk testing looks for variants a person was born with. A tumour result may sometimes prompt a discussion about genetic counselling and a separate inherited-risk test.' },
];
---
<MainLayout title="Precision Medicine" description="Understand liquid biopsy, CTCs, ctDNA, next-generation sequencing and drug sensitivity testing, including their different roles and evidence limitations." breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Precision Medicine' }]}>
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Precision medicine" title="Every Cancer Is Different. Understanding It Matters." description="Learning about a cancer’s features may help your care team evaluate options. The right information starts with the right question." image="/images/cards/laboratory.webp" />
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section intro-split" aria-labelledby="precision-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">A more individual view</p><h2 id="precision-title">What is precision medicine?</h2><p>Precision medicine uses information about a person and their cancer to help guide care. Biomarker tests can look for genes, proteins or other features that may be relevant to a treatment decision.</p><p>In some cancer settings, these tests are part of established care. Other applications are still being researched. The value of a result depends on the specific test and how it is used.</p></div><aside class="info-card"><h3>Three questions to ask</h3><ol class="question-list"><li>What clinical question will this test answer?</li><li>Could the result change my care?</li><li>What evidence supports that change?</li></ol><p class="muted">More testing does not always mean more useful information.</p></aside></section>
    <section class="section section-surface bg-section" aria-labelledby="liquid-title"><p class="eyebrow">Understanding the sample</p><h2 id="liquid-title">Liquid biopsy: a sample, different signals.</h2><p class="section-intro">A liquid biopsy looks for cancer-related material in a body fluid, usually blood. It is an umbrella term, rather than a single test. Two types of material are described below.</p><div class="comparison-grid"><article class="comparison-card"><div class="cell-visual" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div class="comparison-body"><span class="term">CTCs</span><h3>Circulating tumour cells</h3><p>Whole cancer cells that have entered the bloodstream from a tumour.</p><dl><dt>What is examined?</dt><dd>Intact cells, which may be counted or characterised.</dd><dt>Where might it help?</dt><dd>Certain tests have specific prognostic or monitoring uses. Many other applications are investigational.</dd></dl></div></article><article class="comparison-card"><div class="dna-visual" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="180" height="72" viewBox="0 0 180 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M10 18C50 18 45 54 85 54S125 18 170 18M10 54C50 54 45 18 85 18S125 54 170 54"/><path d="M22 20v32m24-20v8m24-19v30m25-32v34m25-24v14m24-24v34m20-33v34" stroke-width="2" opacity=".6"/></svg></div><div class="comparison-body"><span class="term">ctDNA</span><h3>Circulating tumour DNA</h3><p>Small fragments of DNA shed by cancer cells into the bloodstream.</p><dl><dt>What is examined?</dt><dd>Tumour-associated DNA changes using a molecular assay.</dd><dt>Where might it help?</dt><dd>Some tests can identify treatment-relevant changes in defined settings. Other uses, including many recurrence-monitoring applications, require careful evidence review.</dd></dl></div></article></div><p class="note">CTCs and ctDNA are different. Neither is the same as a routine blood count. A negative result does not rule out cancer or all relevant molecular changes.</p></section>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="methods-title"><p class="eyebrow">Understanding the method</p><h2 id="methods-title">Different tools answer different questions.</h2><div class="method-grid"><article class="info-card"><span class="number-label">01 / Molecular testing</span><h3>Next-generation sequencing (NGS)</h3><p>NGS is a laboratory method that reads many DNA or RNA sequences at once. It can be used on tumour tissue or, with an appropriate assay, tumour-derived material in blood.</p><p>It may find changes relevant to an approved treatment or clinical trial. Some findings have uncertain meaning, and some tests find no actionable changes.</p><p class="key-point">NGS describes a testing method. Liquid biopsy describes the source of a sample.</p></article><article class="info-card"><span class="number-label">02 / Functional testing</span><h3>Drug sensitivity testing</h3><p>These laboratory approaches expose collected cancer cells or models to medicines and measure their behaviour outside the body.</p><p>A laboratory response does not establish that a treatment will benefit a patient. Clinical validity and usefulness vary; many approaches remain investigational and should not direct care on their own.</p><p class="key-point">Ask about evidence for the exact assay, cancer type and proposed use.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section evaluation bg-resource" aria-labelledby="evaluation-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">Putting results in context</p><h2 id="evaluation-title">How testing may support clinical evaluation</h2><p>Results are one part of a bigger picture. An oncology team considers them alongside the diagnosis, stage, previous treatments, general health and the available evidence.</p><Button href="/healthcare-professionals/" label="Resources for Professionals" /></div><ol class="evaluation-steps"><li><Icon name="question" size={32}/><h3>Define the question</h3><p>Clarify why a test is being considered and whether it is recommended.</p></li><li><Icon name="microscope" size={32}/><h3>Choose an appropriate test</h3><p>Consider sample quality, limitations, timing, cost and consent.</p></li><li><Icon name="clipboard" size={32}/><h3>Interpret the findings</h3><p>Discuss clinically relevant findings and uncertain or negative results.</p></li><li><Icon name="people" size={32}/><h3>Make a shared decision</h3><p>Review possible options, access, benefits and risks with the treating team.</p></li></ol></section>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="questions-title"><p class="eyebrow">Common questions</p><h2 id="questions-title">Making sense of testing.</h2><FAQ items={faqs} /></section>
    <section class="content-section references" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><h2 id="sources-title">Sources & further reading</h2><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/biomarker-testing-cancer-treatment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Institute: Biomarker testing for cancer treatment</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/types/precision-medicine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Institute: Precision medicine</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/liquid-biopsy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Institute: Liquid biopsy</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/circulating-tumor-cell" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Institute: Circulating tumour cell</a></li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2011.36.0354" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASCO clinical practice guideline on chemotherapy sensitivity and resistance assays (2011)</a></li></ul><p class="note">Educational draft: clinical review and a current evidence check are required before publication. Testing may provide additional information; its clinical relevance depends on the test, the patient and the available evidence. Testing does not guarantee a response or replace standard clinical care.</p></section>
  </div>
</MainLayout>
<style>
.intro-split,.evaluation{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:64px;align-items:start}.section-intro{max-width:75ch;color:#526375;margin-bottom:28px}.question-list{padding-left:20px}.question-list li{padding:10px 0}.comparison-grid,.method-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px}.comparison-card{border:1px solid #dbe8ee;border-radius:24px;overflow:hidden}.comparison-body{padding:30px}.term,.number-label{font-size:.8rem;font-weight:700;color:#007e87;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}.comparison-body h3{margin-top:8px}.comparison-body dt{font-weight:650;margin-top:18px}.comparison-body dd{margin:6px 0 0;color:#526375}.cell-visual,.dna-visual{height:118px;background:#edf8fc;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:18px}.cell-visual span{width:36px;height:36px;border:3px solid #078e99;border-radius:50%;box-shadow:0 0 0 10px #d2eff0;background:#b3e4e3}.cell-visual span:nth-child(2){width:47px;height:47px}.dna-visual{background:#f0eef9;gap:7px;color:#5371b6;font-size:3.5rem;line-height:1}.key-point{padding-top:18px;margin-top:24px;border-top:1px solid #dbe8ee;font-weight:600}.evaluation{padding:38px;border-radius:24px;background:#f2f9fc;margin-top:24px}.evaluation-steps{padding:0;margin:0;list-style:none;counter-reset:steps}.evaluation-steps li{counter-increment:steps;position:relative;padding-left:44px;margin-bottom:24px}.evaluation-steps li::before{content:'0' counter(steps);position:absolute;left:0;top:2px;color:#007e87;font-weight:700}.evaluation-steps h3{font-size:1.1rem;margin:0 0 8px}.evaluation-steps p{margin:0}.references{padding-bottom:50px}.references h2{font-size:1.3rem}@media(max-width:800px){.intro-split,.evaluation{gap:28px}.comparison-body{padding:24px}}@media(max-width:700px){.intro-split,.evaluation,.comparison-grid,.method-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.evaluation{padding:26px}.dna-visual,.cell-visual{height:100px}}
</style>
